For Project Two, I developed an intelligent pirate agent capable of solving a pathfinding problem using deep Q-learning. I was provided with starter code that established the maze environment, 
experience memory, neural network structure, and supporting functions for interacting with the treasure hunt game. 
My primary responsibility was completing the Q-training algorithm that allowed the pirate to learn how to navigate the environment and reach the treasure.

The training algorithm implemented an epsilon-greedy strategy to balance exploration and exploitation. During training, the pirate interacted with the maze, stored its experiences, 
and used those experiences to train the neural network. The model learned to predict Q-values for the available actions and increasingly relied on those predictions as training progressed. 
The completed agent achieved a 100% recent win rate during training and was tested using the provided validation functions.

Computer scientists use computational methods to analyze problems and develop solutions that can be implemented through software and computer systems. 
This involves more than simply writing code. It requires understanding a problem, identifying its constraints, selecting an appropriate approach, implementing a solution, 
and evaluating whether that solution actually accomplishes its intended goal.

This matters because software increasingly makes decisions or supports decisions in areas that affect people directly. 
Artificial intelligence demonstrates this particularly well because an AI system's behavior is influenced by its training process, available information, algorithms, and objectives. 
Computer scientists therefore need to understand not only how to make a system function, but also why it behaves the way it does.

I approach problems by first breaking the larger problem into smaller components that can be understood and tested individually. I identify the expected result, 
determine what information and tools are available, and then develop an implementation that can be evaluated against that goal. If the result is incorrect, 
I use the behavior of the program and available data to determine which part of the implementation needs to be changed.

The reinforcement-learning project demonstrated this process clearly. The objective was for the pirate to reach the treasure, but the solution was not simply a predefined path. 
The problem had to be represented through states, actions, rewards, exploration, exploitation, and repeated training. By evaluating the agent's performance over time, 
I could determine whether the algorithm was actually learning rather than simply executing successfully.

As a computer scientist, I have a responsibility to create systems that are reliable, secure, and designed with consideration for the people affected by them. 
This includes protecting user information, reducing unnecessary risks, testing software appropriately, and being transparent about the capabilities and limitations of a system. 
With artificial intelligence, developers should also consider whether training data, reward structures, or algorithmic decisions could introduce unintended behavior or bias.

There is also a responsibility to the organization developing or deploying the software. A computer scientist should produce maintainable and well-documented solutions, 
communicate technical limitations accurately, protect organizational and user data, and avoid presenting a system as more reliable than testing demonstrates. Ultimately, 
technical success should include both making the software work and ensuring that it behaves responsibly within the environment where it will be used.
